---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cards from '../components/Cards.astro';
import { RELATED, SITE } from '../content/site';
import { longDate } from '../content/dates';
import { SOURCES } from '../content/sources';

const title = 'Privacy Policy — Your Images Never Leave Your Device | AVIFBox';
const description =
  'AVIFBox converts images in your browser: files are never uploaded, and there are no cookies, analytics or ads. What the site and its host, Cloudflare, can see.';
/** The day this policy last changed. */
const updated = '2026-11-15';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/privacy">
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap reveal">
      <p class="label">
        <span>
          <small>Document</small>
          <strong>Privacy policy</strong>
        </span>
        <span>
          <small>Subject</small>
          <strong>Your files</strong>
        </span>
        <span class="barcode" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </p>
      <h1>
        Privacy <em>policy</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The short version: your images are converted on your own device and are never uploaded. There are no cookies, no
        analytics and no ads.
      </p>
      <p class="dateline">
        Updated <time datetime={updated}>{longDate(updated)}</time>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="doc wrap">
    <section class="split">
      <section id="images">
        <div class="sec-head">
          <span class="sec-no">№ 01</span>
          <h2>Your images</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Your browser decodes and re-encodes each image in its own memory. The file is not sent to AVIFBox or to
            anyone else, and nothing is kept: reload or close the page and it is gone.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is built in rather than promised. The site is a set of static files, so there is no upload address to
            send an image to, and each page’s security policy only allows connections to avifbox.com. Your browser’s
            network panel will show that converting sends nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The only things the converter fetches are its own code and, if you press one of the sample buttons, a sample
            image — both from avifbox.com.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="not">
        <div class="sec-head">
          <span class="sec-no">№ 02</span>
          <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>No cookies.</strong> AVIFBox sets none, and saves nothing in your browser’s storage.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>No analytics.</strong> No tracking scripts, pixels or fingerprinting.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>No ads</strong>, and nothing from third parties: scripts, fonts and images all come from
              avifbox.com.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>No accounts</strong>, so there is no profile, password or email address to keep.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>
    </section>

    <section class="split">
      <section id="host">
        <div class="sec-head">
          <span class="sec-no">№ 03</span>
          <h2>What the host can see</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The site is served by Cloudflare. Like any network that delivers a website, it handles every request, and so
            sees the IP address the request came from, the address of the page and the browser’s user-agent. Cloudflare
            uses this to deliver the site and to protect it from abuse, under{' '}
            <a href={SOURCES.cloudflarePrivacy}>its own privacy policy</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            AVIFBox adds nothing to that: it has no server-side code, no database and no logs of its own. The site’s
            operator sees traffic figures in Cloudflare’s dashboard, such as how many requests arrived and from which
            countries.
          </p>
          <p>
            One thing your browser may do by itself: if it fails to reach the site, it can send Cloudflare a report of
            the failed request — the address and the kind of error — so that network faults get noticed. Cloudflare{' '}
            <a href={SOURCES.cloudflareNel}>says it does not store the IP address</a> such a report comes from.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="contact">
        <div class="sec-head">
          <span class="sec-no">№ 04</span>
          <h2>If you write in</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            If you email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>, your address and your message are used to
            answer you, and for nothing else.
          </p>
          <p>
            When a file fails, the tool offers a link that opens a draft in your own mail program, with the page, the
            error message and your browser’s user-agent already written. Nothing is sent unless you send it, and your
            file is not attached.
          </p>
          <p>
            What AVIFBox is and how it works is on the <a href="/about">About page</a>. When this policy changes, the
            date at the top changes with it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </section>

    <Cards
      id="related"
      number="№ 05"
      title="More from the box"
      links={[RELATED.avifToJpg, RELATED.viewer, RELATED.jfifToJpg]}
    />
  </article>
</Base>
